{"format":"hologram.registry.artifact/v1","id":"mcr.microsoft.com/azurelinux/base/valkey","fetched":"2026-09-25T12:12:08.530Z","source":{"name":"Microsoft Artifact Registry","api":"https://mcr.microsoft.com/api/v1/catalog/azurelinux/base/valkey/details?reg=mar","page":"https://mcr.microsoft.com/en-us/product/azurelinux/base/valkey/about"},"readme":"## About\n\nThis repository contains the official Valkey image for Azure Linux.\n\n### What is Azure Linux?\n\nAzure Linux is a Linux distribution for Microsoft’s cloud infrastructure and edge products and services. Azure Linux is designed to provide a consistent platform for these devices and services and will enhance Microsoft’s ability to stay current on Linux updates.  \n\nTo learn more about Azure Linux, refer to our [GitHub repository](https://github.com/microsoft/azurelinux/).\n\n### What is Valkey?\nValkey is an open source (BSD) high-performance key/value datastore that supports a variety of workloads such as caching, message queues, and can act as a primary database. Valkey can run as either a standalone daemon or in a cluster, with options for replication and high availability.\n\nSee: [Valkey](https://valkey.io/)\n\n## Featured tags\n\n- `8`\n\n## Usage\n\n### How to use the image\n\nGenerally, you need to have a docker container that runs Valkey server and then have Valkey client that talks to it. By default, the container ships with both the server and client to be available, so you can run second container with the client that is connected to the server for interfacing, alternatively you can use other means to interface with it.\n\n#### Start a Valkey server instance\n\nTo start a Valkey server instance, create a dedicated network for both the Valkey server and valkey-cli using the command `docker network create my-valkey-network`. Subsequently, ensure connectivity between both containers by utilizing the `--network my-valkey-network` docker parameter. After you have network created/configured, you can start the server using the command:\n\n    docker run --name my-valkey-server --network my-valkey-network -d mcr.microsoft.com/azurelinux/base/valkey:8 valkey-server --protected-mode no\n\n**Note:** For convenience, the above command initializes the Valkey server with protected mode turned off, making it suitable for development and testing purposes. However, it is **strongly** advised to have protected mode turned on and establish a password (via a config file) if you intend to expose your Valkey instance to the internet for enhanced security. Refer to the section [Security](#security) for more details.\n\n#### Connecting via valkey-cli\n\nTo start valkey-cli container in interactive mode and connect it to the server container which was started from section [Start a Valkey Server Instance](#start-a-valkey-server-instance) run the following command:\n\n    docker run -it --network my-valkey-network --rm mcr.microsoft.com/azurelinux/base/valkey:8 valkey-cli -h my-valkey-server\n\nIf you want to run a simple command in valkey-cli to skip the interactive mode, you can always pass the command like this:\n\n    docker run -it --network my-valkey-network --rm mcr.microsoft.com/azurelinux/base/valkey:8 valkey-cli -h my-valkey-server LPUSH mylist a b c d\n\n#### Valkey configuration\nBy default, the image is shipped with default `valkey.conf` file which is located in `/etc/valkey.conf` path. When no arguments are provided to the Valkey server, it will use the `valkey.conf` file as configuration. However, when any argument is provided, the `valkey.conf` file will not be used as the default configuration file which will be indicated by a log message within the server container. You can always provide path for a config file you want the server to use, an example of such a command is:\n\n    docker run --name my-valkey-server --network my-valkey-network -d mcr.microsoft.com/azurelinux/base/valkey:8 valkey-server /path/to/config/container.conf\n\nAlternatively, you can craft your custom image based on the Azure Linux Valkey image, incorporating your specific configuration file:\n\n```\nFROM mcr.microsoft.com/azurelinux/base/valkey:8\nCOPY valkey.conf /etc/valkey/valkey.conf\nCMD [ \"valkey-server\", \"/etc/valkey/valkey.conf\" ]\n```\n\n### Launch Azure Linux shell\n\nThe Valkey image ships with a bash shell. The following command will launch the shell:\n\n    docker run -it --rm mcr.microsoft.com/azurelinux/base/valkey:8 /bin/bash\n\n## Support\n\nAzure Linux container images are supported only for internal Microsoft use. For more information please see our [Support Statement](https://github.com/microsoft/azurelinux/blob/-/SUPPORT.md).\n\n## License\n\nThe Azure Linux distribution is provided under the [MIT license](https://github.com/microsoft/azurelinux/blob/-/LICENSE). Individual packages and components are governed by their own licenses included in the package sources available via srpm on [packages.microsoft.com](https://packages.microsoft.com/azurelinux/) or via our [GitHub repository](https://github.com/microsoft/azurelinux/).\n\n- Legal Notice: [Container License Information](https://aka.ms/mcr/osslegalnotice)","readmeBase":null,"publisher":{"name":"Microsoft","url":"https://mcr.microsoft.com/","official":true,"verified":true},"registered":null,"updated":"2026-09-12T02:12:20.8936819+00:00","categories":[],"keywords":[],"license":"MIT","links":[],"maintainers":[],"stats":{"pulls":null,"pullsText":"12K+","stars":null},"mediaTypes":[],"contentTypes":[],"storage":null,"tags":[{"name":"8.0","digest":"sha256:1f9457e9e325de0301c85d0c18765777820e06290c03849545868b4d22781496","pushed":"2026-09-12T01:33:36.0000000+00:00","size":null,"platforms":[],"mediaType":"application/vnd.docker.distribution.manifest.list.v2+json","status":null},{"name":"8","digest":"sha256:1f9457e9e325de0301c85d0c18765777820e06290c03849545868b4d22781496","pushed":"2026-09-12T01:33:33.0000000+00:00","size":null,"platforms":[],"mediaType":"application/vnd.docker.distribution